---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import SpritesImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp-lua/img/1-20-sprites.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const ByHand = `print(crew[1])
print(crew[2])
print(crew[3])`;

const ByIndex = `for i = 1, #crew do
    print(crew[i])
end`;

const ByIpairs = `for i, name in ipairs(crew) do
    print(i, name)
end`;

const IpairsOut = `1	Ada
2	Bo
3	Cy`;

const TwoNames = `for i, name in ipairs(crew) do`;

const Parallel = `local xs = {120, 260, 400, 540, 680}
local ys = {180, 320, 240, 430, 200}`;

const Sprites = `local xs = {120, 260, 400, 540, 680}
local ys = {180, 320, 240, 430, 200}

function love.draw()
    for i, x in ipairs(xs) do
        love.graphics.setColor(0.3, 0.6 + i / 20, 1)
        love.graphics.circle("fill", x, ys[i], 34)
    end
end`;

const Gappy = `local gappy = {"a", nil, "c"}

for i, v in ipairs(gappy) do
    print(i, v)
end

print("#gappy is " .. #gappy)`;

const GappyOut = `1	a
#gappy is 1`;

const NotationLua = `local crew = {"Ada", "Bo", "Cy"}

for i = 1, #crew do
    print(crew[i])
end`;

const NotationPseudo = `crew ← ["Ada", "Bo", "Cy"]
i ← 1

REPEAT LENGTH(crew) TIMES
{
  DISPLAY(crew[i])
  i ← i + 1
}`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.20: Going Through a Table</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.20</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Going Through a Table</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>A Loop and a Table Fit Together</p>
              <p>Two Ways to Walk a List</p>
              <p>N Things From N Values</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What You Are Building</h2>
              <Image
                src={SpritesImage}
                alt="Five blue circles scattered at different heights across a black window"
                width={520}
                class="img-fluid rounded"
              />
              <p class="mt-3">
                Five circles in five places, from{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">two tables and one loop.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>You Have Both Halves Already</h2>
              <Code code={ByHand} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>You Have Both Halves Already</h2>
              <Code code={ByHand} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p>
                A table holds many values.{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">A loop counts.</b> The index is the
                count.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Which Is One Line</h2>
              <Code code={ByIndex} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Which Is One Line</h2>
              <Code code={ByIndex} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p>
                <code>#crew</code> from last session is the end of the count, so
                the loop{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">fits the table however long it is.</b>
              </p>
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                Add a fourth name and nothing else changes.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Traversal</h2>
              <p>
                Visiting every element of a list, in order.{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">The exam's word, and it appears
                constantly.</b>
              </p>
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                Almost everything anyone does with a list is a traversal with
                one line changed in the middle.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Lua Has a Shorter Way</h2>
              <Code code={ByIpairs} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Lua Has a Shorter Way</h2>
              <Code code={ByIpairs} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <Code code={IpairsOut} frame="terminal" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Names, Not One</h2>
              <Code code={TwoNames} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>i</dt>
                  <dd>Which one you are on. 1, then 2, then 3.</dd>
                  <dt>name</dt>
                  <dd>The value itself. You do not have to look it up.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                Same idea as the mouse giving you two answers at session 13.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which One Should You Use?</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>for i = 1, #t</dt>
                  <dd>When you need the index for something else &mdash; a second table, a position, a count.</dd>
                  <dt>ipairs</dt>
                  <dd>When you only want the values.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                <code>ipairs</code> gives you both, so it is rarely wrong. The
                indexed one is what the exam looks like.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Tables, One Index</h2>
              <Code code={Parallel} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p>
                <code>xs[3]</code> and <code>ys[3]</code> are{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">the same circle.</b> The index is what
                connects them.
              </p>
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                Parallel lists. Nothing in the code says they belong together
                &mdash; you have to keep them the same length yourself.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Traverse a table with an indexed loop</li>
                <li>Traverse a table with <code>ipairs</code></li>
                <li>Use one index across two tables</li>
                <li>Draw N shapes from N stored values</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Traversal</dt>
                  <dd>Visiting every element of a list, in order.</dd>
                  <dt>ipairs</dt>
                  <dd>A loop that hands you the index and the value together.</dd>
                  <dt>Parallel lists</dt>
                  <dd>Two lists where the same index means the same thing in both.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 1: Both Ways</h3>

          <p>
            Copy <CodeSnippet code="template-console" lang="txt" inline /> to{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="1-20-walk" lang="txt" inline />. Make a table of
            five things &mdash; anything &mdash; and print it twice, once each
            way:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={`local crew = {"Ada", "Bo", "Cy", "Dee", "Eli"}

for i = 1, #crew do
    print(i .. ": " .. crew[i])
end

print("---")

for i, name in ipairs(crew) do
    print(i .. ": " .. name)
end`}
            lang="lua"
            frame="code"
            title="main.lua"
          />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The output is identical.</b> Then add a
            sixth name and run it again without touching either loop.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 2: Do Something to Each One</h3>

          <p>
            Make a table of five numbers. In one traversal, print each number
            doubled. In another, add them all up and print the total:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={`local scores = {12, 8, 21, 3, 17}
local total = 0

for i, score in ipairs(scores) do
    total = total + score
end

print("Total: " .. total)
print("Count: " .. #scores)
print("Average: " .. total / #scores)`}
            lang="lua"
            frame="code"
            title="main.lua"
          />

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b><CodeSnippet code="local total = 0" lang="lua" inline /> goes above the loop, not inside it.</b>{" "}
            This is the same rule as session 15's <code>tries</code> and session
            14's <code>hits</code>. <b class="text-sn-theme">Third time; it
            should be a habit by now.</b>
          </div>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 3: Five Circles From Two Tables</h3>

          <p>
            Copy <CodeSnippet code="template-game" lang="txt" inline /> to{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="1-20-sprites" lang="txt" inline />:
          </p>

          <Code code={Sprites} lang="lua" frame="code" title="main.lua" mark={{ range: "5-8" }} />

          <Image
            src={SpritesImage}
            alt="Five circles at the positions stored in the two tables"
            width={520}
            class="img-fluid rounded my-3"
          />

          <p>
            Then <b class="text-sn-theme">add a sixth position to both tables</b>{" "}
            and run it. A sixth circle appears and you changed no code.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 4: Break the Pair on Purpose</h3>

          <p>
            Add a value to <code>xs</code> and <b>not</b> to <code>ys</code>.
            Run it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>The window turns blue.</b> Copy the message and work out which
            table the loop was walking, and which one ran out.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Then do the opposite &mdash; add to <code>ys</code> and not{" "}
            <code>xs</code>. <b class="text-sn-theme">This one does not
            crash</b>, and that is worse. Write down what you see and why.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 5: Make Them Yours</h3>

          <p>
            Add a third parallel table and use it for something &mdash; radius,
            color, or both. Then pick one:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Make one circle in the list move, using its index to pick which</li>
            <li>Draw a line from each circle to the next one</li>
            <li>Use the index to number the circles on screen</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Challenge (Optional): The Hole</h3>

          <p>Back in the terminal:</p>

          <Code code={Gappy} lang="lua" frame="code" title="main.lua" />

          <Code
            code={GappyOut}
            lang="txt"
            frame="terminal"
            title="Terminal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-2"
          />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The <code>"c"</code> is in the table and
            the traversal never reached it.</b> So did <code>#gappy</code>.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is last session's warning with a loop attached: a table with a
            gap in it is not a list, and every tool that walks a list stops at
            the hole. <b>Write down what this means for a table you build by
            deleting things out of the middle.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Exam notation -->
        <section id="exam-notation">
          <h2>Same Idea, Exam Notation</h2>

          <p>
            The exam traverses lists constantly, and it does it with the loop
            that has no counter &mdash; so a counter gets built by hand.
          </p>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationLua}
                frame="code"
                lang="lua"
                title="Lua"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationPseudo}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Exam Notation"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th></th>
                  <th>Lua</th>
                  <th>Exam notation</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>How many elements</td>
                  <td><code>#crew</code></td>
                  <td><code>LENGTH(crew)</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>The first one</td>
                  <td><code>crew[1]</code></td>
                  <td><code>crew[1]</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Walk the whole list</td>
                  <td><code>for i = 1, #crew do</code></td>
                  <td><code>REPEAT LENGTH(crew) TIMES</code></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b>There is no <code>ipairs</code> on the exam.</b> It is a Lua
            convenience, and a good one. Use it in your own programs and expect
            the indexed form on the exam.
          </div>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The index still starts at 1 in both</b>,
            which is the agreement from session 18 doing real work. A traversal
            written in Lua and the same traversal in exam notation visit the
            same elements in the same order.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <CodeSnippet code="1-20-walk" lang="txt" inline /> prints the same
              list two different ways
            </li>
            <li>Adding a sixth item changed no loop</li>
            <li>
              <CodeSnippet code="1-20-sprites" lang="txt" inline /> draws its
              circles from two parallel tables
            </li>
            <li>Both mismatch experiments from task 4 are written down</li>
            <li>A third parallel table is doing something</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              You added a sixth circle by adding two numbers and changing no
              code. Explain what makes that possible, and describe what you
              would have had to do in session 2 to add a sixth shape.
            </li>
            <li>
              A longer <code>xs</code> crashed and a longer <code>ys</code> did
              not. Explain the difference, and say which of the two you would
              rather have happen in a program you were about to hand in.
            </li>
            <li>
              Nothing in the code says <code>xs</code> and <code>ys</code>{" "}
              belong together. Explain what actually connects them, and describe
              one way that could go wrong that Lua would never warn you about.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
